Merger Agreement: "Material Detriment" shall mean any change, event, or occurrence that, whether considered individually or in aggregate, either materially impairs the Company's capacity to consummate the Transaction or negatively impacts its overall financial and operational status. Notwithstanding this, no change, event, or occurrence shall trigger a finding of Material Detriment if it arises solely from: (i) normal fluctuations in market conditions or currency exchange rates; (ii) legislative or regulatory modifications that apply uniformly to all industry participants; (iii) actions attributable to the public announcement or preliminary negotiations of this Agreement; or (iv) force majeure events, including natural disasters and pandemics, provided these do not disproportionately affect the Company compared to its industry peers. Accordingly, any impact on the Company's ability to consummate the Transaction that is exclusively due to the factors in clauses (i) through (iv) shall be excluded from a Material Detriment determination. Question: Is the "ability to consummate" concept within this Agreement subject to the carveouts outlined above?
