Merger Agreement Excerpt: 

'Material Adverse Effect' shall mean any event, change, condition, or development that, individually or in aggregate, (a) would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse impact on the business, financial condition, assets, or overall operational performance of the Company; or (b) would, either directly or indirectly, prevent, materially delay, or materially impair the ability of the Company to consummate the Transaction. Notwithstanding the foregoing, the following events shall not be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect for the purposes of this Agreement: 
   (i) any fluctuations in general economic or capital market conditions, including changes in prevailing interest rates or currency exchange rates, unless such fluctuations result in an impact that is disproportionate relative to other similarly situated industry participants; 
   (ii) any modifications to applicable laws, regulations, or accounting standards, or changes in their interpretation by governmental authorities, provided that such modifications do not single-handedly impede the consummation of the Transaction; 
   (iii) any force majeure event, including but not limited to pandemics, natural disasters, or other weather-related events, except where such events directly cause a failure of essential operational functions; and 
   (iv) any routine adjustments or reclassifications in financial reporting. 

Question: Based on the above definition, is the 'ability to consummate' aspect explicitly subject to carveouts under the Material Adverse Effect provisions?
Choices:
